APP_NAME=waterfall
APP_ENV=dev
APP_HOST=0.0.0.0
APP_PORT=8000
APP_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://waterfall:waterfall@localhost:5432/waterfall
# SECRET_KEY=<set locally or via a secret manager>
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
NEXT_ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=localhost,127.0.0.1
WF_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
WF_ADMIN_IS_ACTIVE=true
# WF_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# Read only by docker-compose.full.yml -- but that file is interpolated by every stop and
# cleanup target too (`make down`, `make stop`, `make clean-docker`, `make distclean`, all
# of which use the full compose), not just by `make up-full`. So these two are required
# even in a native-dev setup that never starts pgAdmin or Grafana: without them, `make
# down` fails on a service that was never running.
# PGADMIN_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# Required one level earlier than the pgAdmin/Grafana secrets above: the *base* compose
# file declares REDIS_PASSWORD without a default, and Compose interpolates the whole file
# before selecting services, so `make db-up` and `make logs` also fail until this is set.
# REDIS_PASSWORD=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# Defaults to memory:// -- an in-process limiter needing no server, like DATABASE_URL
# defaulting to SQLite. Counters are then neither shared between instances nor kept across a
# restart, so set a real redis:// URL anywhere that runs more than one process. In Docker the
# compose file sets both for you. Keep the password out of the URL -- it is not
# percent-encoded.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0
# Object storage for the uploaded MS Project sources (Garage). Required exactly like
# REDIS_PASSWORD above -- the base compose file declares the three without a default, and
# Compose interpolates the whole file before selecting services, so `make db-up` and
# `make logs` fail until they are set. Garage enforces the format of both S3
# credentials: the access key ID is `GK` followed by 24 hex characters, the secret key is
# exactly 64 hex characters. Generate a set with:
#   echo "GARAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=GK$(openssl rand -hex 12)"
#   echo "GARAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
#   echo "GARAGE_RPC_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
# The access key ID cannot be reused with a different secret afterwards: Garage never
# reassigns a key ID, so changing the secret means choosing a new ID too.
# GARAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# GARAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# GARAGE_RPC_SECRET=<set locally or via a secret manager>
# Native dev only (Configuration A): in Docker the compose file points the API at the
# `garage` service itself. GARAGE_BUCKET has a default and only needs setting to use
# another bucket name.
# GARAGE_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:3900
# GARAGE_BUCKET=waterfall-imports
# SigV4 signs the region into the credential scope, so this must match the store's own
# region: `s3_region` in infra/docker/garage/garage.toml for the bundled Garage (already
# the default below), or the bucket's region when pointing at MinIO or AWS S3 instead. A
# mismatch fails every call with an opaque SignatureDoesNotMatch.
# GARAGE_REGION=garage
# ADMIN_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
